Interspecific competition occurs when members of different species interact and negatively affect one another's ability to survive and reproduce. The principle of competitive exclusion states that in an environment where two species are competing for the same resources, only the species naturally best endowed to utilize those resources will eventually survive. Coexistence by so-called competitive release does occur (Krebs 1968), but this is conditional upon there being redundancies in the use of the resources (Schoener, 1983) and onto mutually beneficial changes in behavior by both species in an effort to avoid damaging the other (Lima & Dill, 1990). The answers is (G).
