The absorption coefficient for a surface is equal to 1 minus the reflectivity. So if we have two walls with reflectivity $r_1$ and $r_2$, the fraction of light that gets through is exactly $(1-r_1)(1-r_2)$ (the fraction that gets through to the end of the second layer and then back through the first again). If the reflectivity is 0.9, the transmission (and absorption for one reflection) coefficient is $(1-0.9)(1-0.9) = 0.01$. If the reflection goes down to 0.3, the transmission (and absorption for one reflection) coefficient will be $(1-0.3)(1-0.3) = 0.49$. So the increase in transmission (and absorption) is 49. So the answer is about 50, which is (A).
